---
import DocShell from '../../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../../lib/nav';
import { getPracticeURL, withPracticeURL } from '../../lib/practice';
import batch from '../../samples/sr-batch.txt?raw';
import elementsHeading from '../../samples/sr-elements-heading.txt?raw';
import expectPass from '../../samples/sr-expect-pass.txt?raw';
import goto from '../../samples/sr-goto.txt?raw';
import next from '../../samples/sr-next.txt?raw';
import readFull from '../../samples/sr-read-full.txt?raw';
import start from '../../samples/sr-start.txt?raw';
import transcriptDialog from '../../samples/sr-transcript-dialog.txt?raw';
import walkFixed from '../../samples/sr-walk-fixed.txt?raw';

const practiceURL = getPracticeURL(Astro);
const fixedURL = `${practiceURL}fixed/`;
const batchInput = [
   `a1 sr batch <<'EOF'`,
   '{"action":"goto","payload":{"role":"button","name":"Invite member"}}',
   '{"action":"checkpoint","payload":{"label":"opened"}}',
   '{"action":"activate"}',
   '{"action":"expect","payload":{"match":"dialog, Invite member","since":"opened"}}',
   'EOF',
].join('\n');
const toc = [
   { id: 'session', label: 'Start a session' },
   { id: 'move', label: 'Move and read' },
   { id: 'jump', label: 'Jump, list, and find' },
   { id: 'page', label: 'Read the whole page' },
   { id: 'controls', label: 'Use controls' },
   { id: 'check', label: 'Check an announcement' },
   { id: 'transcript', label: 'Save the transcript' },
   { id: 'batch', label: 'Run steps from a file' },
   { id: 'commands', label: 'Screen reader commands' },
   { id: 'native', label: 'VoiceOver and NVDA' },
   { id: 'stop', label: 'Stop the session' },
   { id: 'browser-support', label: 'Readers and browsers' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Test with a screen reader"
   summary="<p>Start a session, move through the page the way a screen reader user does, and check what was announced. The same commands drive VoiceOver, NVDA, and the simulated screen reader.</p>"
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section>
      <h2 id="session">Start a session</h2>
      <p>
         A session is a running screen reader. One session is active at a time, and every
         <code>a1 sr</code> command controls it. <code>--sr</code> picks the screen reader.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>--sr virtual</code></dt>
         <dd>The simulated screen reader. It runs in a headless browser on any machine and needs no setup.</dd>
         <dt><code>--sr voiceover</code></dt>
         <dd>VoiceOver, on macOS. It needs <code>a1 setup</code> once.</dd>
         <dt><code>--sr nvda</code></dt>
         <dd>NVDA, on Windows. It needs <code>a1 setup</code> once.</dd>
      </dl>
      <pre><code>a1 sr start --sr virtual {fixedURL}</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{withPracticeURL(start, fixedURL)}</code></pre>
      <p>
         The examples on this page use the docs' <a href="/guides/demo">practice page</a>, a
         demo team settings screen.
      </p>
      <p class="note">
         Without <code>--sr</code>, <code>sr start</code> uses VoiceOver or NVDA when the
         machine is set up for it, and the simulated screen reader otherwise. Pass
         <code>--sr</code> every time so you know which one you get.
      </p>
      <p>
         See <a href="/screen-readers">screen readers</a> for how the three differ and when to
         use each.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="move">Move and read</h2>
      <p>
         A screen reader has a cursor. It sits on one element at a time: a heading, a link, a
         button, a paragraph. Each time the cursor moves, the screen reader announces the
         element it reached.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr next
a1 sr next heading</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{next.trimEnd()}</code></pre>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>Phrase</code></dt>
         <dd>The announcement: what the screen reader said.</dd>
         <dt><code>Item</code></dt>
         <dd>The text of the element. A landmark such as <code>banner</code> has none.</dd>
         <dt><code>Role</code>, <code>Name</code></dt>
         <dd>What the element is and what it is called. A control with a role and no name is a defect.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p><code>sr read</code> prints the element under the cursor without moving.</p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr read</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{readFull.trimEnd()}</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>Source</code> says where the role and name came from. The simulated screen
         reader reads them from the page. VoiceOver and NVDA report an announcement, and a1
         derives the role and name from it.
      </p>
      <p>
         The last three lines report what a1 could observe about keyboard focus and the
         window. Every command prints them. The simulated screen reader has no window, so
         they always say <code>unsupported</code> or <code>unavailable</code> here, and the
         other examples in these docs omit them.
      </p>
      <p>To print the announcement alone, on one line, add <code>--phrase</code>.</p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="jump">Jump, list, and find</h2>
      <p>
         Screen reader users rarely read a page element by element. They jump by heading,
         link, or landmark, and so can you.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr next heading --level 2
a1 sr previous link
a1 sr next button --times 3</code></pre>
      <p>
         The kinds are <code>heading</code>, <code>link</code>, <code>landmark</code>,
         <code>control</code>, <code>button</code>, <code>table</code>, <code>list</code>,
         <code>graphic</code>, <code>region</code>, and <code>form-field</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>sr elements</code> lists every element of one kind from the top of the page,
         like the VoiceOver rotor or the NVDA elements list.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr elements heading</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{elementsHeading.trimEnd()}</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>sr goto</code> moves forward to the next element with a role, a name, or both.
         <code>sr find</code> moves to the next element whose text contains a string. Both
         exit 4 when nothing matches.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr goto --role button --name "Invite member"
a1 sr find "Marcus"</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{goto.trimEnd()}</code></pre>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>sr top</code>, <code>sr bottom</code></dt>
         <dd>Move to the first or last element of the page, or of the group the cursor is in.</dd>
         <dt><code>sr title</code></dt>
         <dd>Print the page title. On VoiceOver and NVDA this is the window title the screen reader reports.</dd>
         <dt><code>sr table &lt;move&gt;</code></dt>
         <dd>Inside a table, move with <code>next-cell</code>, <code>previous-cell</code>, <code>next-row</code>, <code>previous-row</code>, <code>next-column</code>, or <code>previous-column</code>. <code>row-header</code> and <code>column-header</code> print the headers of the current cell on the simulated screen reader. VoiceOver has no row-header command, and NVDA speaks headers as the cursor enters a cell, so those moves exit 2 there.</dd>
         <dt><code>sr open &lt;url&gt;</code></dt>
         <dd>Load another page in the same session.</dd>
      </dl>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="page">Read the whole page</h2>
      <p>
         <code>sr walk</code> reads a page from top to bottom and prints the transcript: a
         log of everything the screen reader announced. It starts a session when none is
         active.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr walk {fixedURL} --sr virtual --max 30</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{withPracticeURL(walkFixed, practiceURL)}</code></pre>
      <p>
         Each line is one stop of the cursor and what the screen reader said there. The text
         in parentheses is the element's own text. Check three things in a transcript.
      </p>
      <ul>
         <li>Every control has a name. A line that reads <code>button</code> alone is a defect.</li>
         <li>The order matches the order on screen.</li>
         <li>States that matter are announced, such as <code>pressed</code> on lines 19 to 21.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
         <code>--max</code> caps the number of stops, 500 by default. <code>--out walk.md</code>
         writes the transcript to a file. <code>sr read-all</code> does the same from wherever
         the cursor is, in a session you already started.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="controls">Use controls</h2>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>sr activate</code></dt>
         <dd>Press the element under the cursor, as Enter or a click would.</dd>
         <dt><code>sr press Tab Tab</code></dt>
         <dd>Press keys in order, one chord per argument: <code>Shift+Tab</code>, <code>ArrowDown</code>, <code>VO+ArrowRight</code>. See <a href="/reference/cli#keys">key names</a>.</dd>
         <dt><code>sr type "text"</code></dt>
         <dd>Type into the focused field.</dd>
         <dt><code>sr escape</code></dt>
         <dd>Press Escape, which closes a menu or a dialog.</dd>
         <dt><code>sr interact</code>, <code>sr stop-interacting</code></dt>
         <dd>Step into a group or control and back out, as VoiceOver does.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         On the simulated screen reader, <code>activate</code> waits for the page to finish
         reacting before it returns, including a page that loads its next view without a full
         navigation. It also presses a control that another element is drawn over, as a real
         screen reader does.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="check">Check an announcement</h2>
      <p>
         To test what one action announces, mark the transcript, do the action, then check
         what came after the mark.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr goto --role button --name "Invite member"
a1 sr checkpoint opened
a1 sr activate
a1 sr expect dialog --since opened</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{expectPass.trimEnd()}</code></pre>
      <p>
         Set the checkpoint before the action. An announcement made before the checkpoint
         does not count.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>sr expect &lt;text&gt;</code></dt>
         <dd>Passes when an announcement contains the text, ignoring case. <code>/pattern/flags</code> matches a regular expression. <code>--not</code> passes when nothing matches. Exits 4 on failure.</dd>
         <dt><code>sr wait --for &lt;text&gt;</code></dt>
         <dd>Waits for an announcement that has not happened yet, such as a message that appears after a network request. Exits 4 at <code>--timeout</code>, 5000 ms by default. <code>sr wait --ms 500</code> only pauses.</dd>
         <dt><code>sr checkpoint &lt;label&gt;</code></dt>
         <dd>Marks a point in the transcript for <code>--since</code>.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         The simulated screen reader announces the role first: <code>button, Pay</code>, not
         <code>Pay, button</code>. Match on the part you care about.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="transcript">Save the transcript</h2>
      <p>The transcript is everything the screen reader has said in this session.</p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr transcript --since opened</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{withPracticeURL(transcriptDialog, practiceURL)}</code></pre>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>--since &lt;checkpoint&gt;</code></dt>
         <dd>Only what came after a checkpoint.</dd>
         <dt><code>--tail &lt;count&gt;</code></dt>
         <dd>Only the last announcements.</dd>
         <dt><code>--out &lt;path&gt;</code></dt>
         <dd>Write a file: Markdown for <code>.md</code>, JSON for <code>.json</code>.</dd>
         <dt><code>--after-index</code>, <code>--limit</code></dt>
         <dd>Page through a long transcript. With <code>--json</code>, <code>transcript.window.nextAfterIndex</code> is where the next page starts.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         <code>a1 sr tail</code> prints announcements as they happen, like
         <code>tail -f</code>. Run it in a second terminal while you work in the first.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="batch">Run steps from a file</h2>
      <p>
         <code>sr batch</code> reads one step per line from a file or from standard input and
         runs them over one connection. It stops at the first failed <code>expect</code> and
         exits 4, unless you pass <code>--continue</code>.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{batchInput}</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{batch.trimEnd()}</code></pre>
      <p>
         Each line is the JSON the MCP <code>sr_action</code> tool takes, or an
         <code>expect</code>. Lines that start with <code>#</code> are comments.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="commands">Screen reader commands</h2>
      <p>
         VoiceOver and NVDA have hundreds of commands of their own. <code>sr list</code>
         searches them, and <code>sr do</code> runs one by name.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr list --query heading
a1 sr do next-heading</code></pre>
      <p>
         VoiceOver has two command sets. a1 uses the first unless you ask for the second.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>voiceover-commander</code></dt>
         <dd>Tells VoiceOver directly what to do. It does not depend on the keyboard layout or on which window has focus. Use it for scripted runs.</dd>
         <dt><code>voiceover-keycode</code></dt>
         <dd>Presses the keys a person would, such as Control-Option-Right Arrow. The browser window must be in front. Use it when the keystroke itself is what you are testing.</dd>
      </dl>
      <pre><code>a1 sr do move-right
a1 sr do voiceover-keycode:move-to-next
a1 sr press "VO+ArrowRight"</code></pre>
      <p>
         The simulated screen reader takes the portable commands only: the ones that also
         exist as an <code>a1 sr</code> subcommand, such as <code>next-heading</code>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="native">VoiceOver and NVDA</h2>
      <p>
         With a real screen reader, a1 opens the URL in a browser, waits for that window to
         come to the front, and starts the screen reader.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr start --sr voiceover http://localhost:3000/checkout
a1 sr start --sr voiceover --browser Safari http://localhost:3000/checkout
a1 sr start --sr voiceover --app Calendar</code></pre>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>--browser &lt;name&gt;</code></dt>
         <dd><code>chrome</code>, <code>edge</code>, <code>brave</code>, <code>chromium</code>, or an app name such as <code>Safari</code>.</dd>
         <dt><code>--app &lt;name&gt;</code></dt>
         <dd>Read a native app that is already open, in place of a page.</dd>
         <dt><code>--recording &lt;path&gt;</code></dt>
         <dd>Record the screen. See <a href="/guides/recording">record a session</a>.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         A real screen reader acts on whatever window is in front. Before each command, a1
         checks that the window it opened is still there. If you clicked somewhere else, run
         <code>a1 sr focus</code> to bring the window back, then <code>a1 sr read</code> to see
         where the cursor is.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>--native-input development</code> turns that check off.
         <code>--native-input require-binding</code> is stricter: it sends nothing unless a1
         can verify which document the screen reader's cursor is in. VoiceOver and NVDA do
         not report that today, so in this mode no input is sent.
      </p>
      <p>
         On VoiceOver, <code>a1 sr screenshot &lt;path&gt;</code> saves a picture of the
         element under the cursor.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="stop">Stop the session</h2>
      <pre><code>a1 sr stop --out transcript.md</code></pre>
      <p>
         Stopping quits the screen reader and finishes any recording. <code>--out</code>
         writes the transcript as the session stops. Stop every VoiceOver or NVDA session you
         start: until you do, the screen reader stays under a1's control.
      </p>
      <p>
         A session ends after 30 minutes without a command. Change that with
         <code>--idle-timeout &lt;minutes&gt;</code> on <code>sr start</code>, or turn it off
         with <code>0</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
         When a stop fails, the error names a session ID. To try again, run
         <code>a1 sr stop --session-id &lt;id&gt;</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
         For one action with no session, add <code>--ephemeral</code> to <code>press</code>,
         <code>type</code>, or <code>do</code>. It starts a session, runs the action, and stops.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="browser-support">Readers and browsers</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
         <table>
            <thead>
               <tr><th scope="col">Screen reader</th><th scope="col">Browser</th><th scope="col">Notes</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
               <tr><th scope="row">VoiceOver, macOS</th><td>Chrome, Edge, Brave, Chromium, Safari</td><td>Pick Safari with <code>--browser Safari</code>. Scans always run in a Chromium browser, so a scan says nothing about Safari.</td></tr>
               <tr><th scope="row">NVDA, Windows</th><td>Chrome, Edge, Brave, Chromium</td><td>Runs on Windows only.</td></tr>
               <tr><th scope="row">Simulated, any system</th><td>Headless Chromium</td><td>Without a Chromium browser it falls back to jsdom, where page scripts do not run.</td></tr>
               <tr><th scope="row">TalkBack, Android</th><td>Any app</td><td>Not an <code>--sr</code> choice. Use <a href="/reference/cli#talkback"><code>a1 talkback</code></a>.</td></tr>
               <tr><th scope="row">Anything else</th><td>Including Firefox</td><td>Not supported.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
         </table>
      </div>
      <p>
         <code>a1 doctor</code> prints the browser version it found. That confirms the browser
         is installed. It does not show that a site works with a screen reader in that
         browser. Test the build you care about and keep the transcript.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
